{"skill":{"slug":"codex-sub-agents","displayName":"OpenAI Codex Sub Agents"},"version":{"version":"1.0.0","createdAt":1769484036381,"changelog":"- Updated skill.","changelogSource":"user","license":null,"files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":7936,"sha256":"8fdc98c3f7d9b82bea312a20939aa73ff29ccd57a8960c8fe905f2d593714c1b","contentType":"text/x-markdown"},{"path":"cli-reference.md","size":3689,"sha256":"5efac8c51f3a5c63cf6ac2bebbf520e492ede171a397b7c2c78a4a856ca6b8d5","contentType":"text/x-markdown"},{"path":"clawdbot-integration.md","size":5889,"sha256":"47a2387855055adb5c938ab0625b97091bb1010b134d289f1136db763361eb21","contentType":"text/x-markdown"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2295,"sha256":"dfce06b8addc2f2d056b0ca120312e1d3b16e72c5807bd93f008fdc64ac20388","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789021775349,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"63f861ce3828b2e0d8dcfd6779333f12ac3880ea6ecdf9b8af6d86b31f08e723","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/63f861ce3828b2e0d8dcfd6779333f12ac3880ea6ecdf9b8af6d86b31f08e723","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: codex-sub-agents\nVersion: 1.0.0\n\nThe skill bundle introduces the `codex CLI` tool, which grants extensive local system access, including arbitrary command execution and file system modifications. The `SKILL.md` and `clawdbot-integration.md` files explicitly instruct the OpenClaw agent to use `codex exec --full-auto`, enabling workspace writes and command execution without explicit approval. Furthermore, the documentation (`SKILL.md`, `cli-reference.md`) details highly privileged flags like `--sandbox danger-full-access` (full machine access) and `--yolo` (no approvals or sandbox), making the agent aware of these dangerous capabilities. While there's no clear evidence of intentional malicious behavior like data exfiltration to external endpoints, the broad system access and instruction to use high-privilege commands without explicit user approval classify this as suspicious due to significant inherent risks.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779160090081},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":100,"severity":"CRITICAL","recommendation":"DO_NOT_INSTALL","issueCount":24,"checkedAt":1789021775349},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"This guide is not malware, but it gives agents broad automatic coding powers and persistent credential/config access, so users should review it before installing.","dimensions":[{"detail":"File editing and command execution are expected for a coding assistant, but examples include full-auto, yolo/danger-full-access, network enablement, additional writable roots, and model/provider routing that are broader than a least-privilege default.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"Installation is disclosed and user-directed through npm and Codex login, but the optional MCP example uses an unpinned npx package and should be treated as third-party code execution.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The trigger list is broad and the Clawdbot/AGENTS examples normalize autonomous Codex execution for coding tasks without requiring explicit user confirmation, path limits, diff review, or test verification before changes are made.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The artifacts reference persistent Codex configuration, global prompts, sessions, auth files, and Clawdbot token sync from ~/.codex/auth.json, which are sensitive cross-session control and credential surfaces.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The purpose is coherent for a Codex CLI coding-agent skill, but the artifact repeatedly documents write-capable and command-executing modes such as codex exec --full-auto, subagents with exec/write/edit/process tools, MCP server mode, and full machine/network access.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"concern"}],"guidance":"Install only if you intend to delegate coding work to Codex CLI. Prefer read-only or approval-required modes first, avoid --full-auto and --yolo except in isolated trusted workspaces, review any changes to ~/.codex or Clawdbot auth/config files, and pin or vet any third-party MCP servers before enabling them.","findings":"[E2] expected: The artifact reads only the named OPENAI_API_KEY for Codex login rather than harvesting the whole environment, so this finding is downgraded to a disclosed authentication example.\n[EA5] expected: Using Codex/OpenAI models is central to the skill, but repeated codex exec examples combined with --full-auto and Clawdbot routing create a real Review concern about off-box processing and unattended local changes.\n[EA2] unexpected: Autonomous modes are documented as normal integration patterns, including standing AGENTS.md and backend examples, without adequate confirmation or review gates.\n[SQP-1] unexpected: The skill's broad triggers such as codex, code review, implement feature, and coding agent could activate a high-authority coding workflow for ordinary requests.\n[SQP-2] unexpected: The concerns about --full-auto, full access, yolo, and log feedback are supported by the artifact; the main issue is under-disclosure and weak scoping, not hidden malicious behavior.\n[AS1] expected: References to ~/.codex/config.toml, ~/.codex/prompts, sessions, and auth files are relevant to Codex setup, but they are persistent and security-sensitive and should be reviewed carefully.\n[AS2] expected: MCP listing and server setup are expected integration features; the artifact does not show secret scraping from mcp.json, but MCP configuration still expands tool access.\n[RA2] expected: Reusable prompts in ~/.codex/prompts are a legitimate Codex feature, but they persist across sessions and can affect future agent behavior.\n[RP1] unexpected: The unpinned npx @anthropic/mcp-server-github example is optional, but it introduces avoidable supply-chain risk in an already high-authority integration.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789021775349}}}}}